# 📜 Source Control API Protocol (L8)

**Domain:** `Core_Strategy`  
**Version:** 2.0 (2026-03-15)  
**Applies To:** All vrvish Agents  

---

## 1. The Catastrophic Failure Rule
The Minisforum AgentHouse is an execution node, **not a vault**. Relying purely on local storage for critical infrastructure (`C:\GitHub\...`) is an unacceptable operational risk. A single misconfigured script, hard drive failure, or destructive `rmdir` command can instantly wipe out weeks of work.

## 2. Mandatory Git Synchronisation (The API-First Mandate)
Every single application, platform, and shared strategy folder MUST be version-controlled and continuously pushed to the remote `vrvish` GitHub organisation.

**The Rule:**
Agents are **strictly prohibited** from using raw `git` CLI commands (`git add`, `git commit`, `git push`) spawned via `run_command` in PowerShell unless absolutely necessary. Raw CLI commands are prone to interactive authentication hangs, parsing errors, and untrackable failures that stall the Swarm queue.

All active Swarm agents **must** use our dedicated Git Node.js API endpoints or the designated canonical scripts (defined in `scripts.json`) to perform source control operations. 

> [!CAUTION]
> **GitHub PAT Automation**
> The underlying API engines rely on the local `C:\GitHub\T1-vrvish-core\theHive\.env` file containing a valid `GITHUB_PAT`. If this token is missing, the API endpoints will fail. The Commander *must* populate the PAT in the `.env` file or securely manage it via the Windows Credential Manager.

## 3. Implementation Logic
1. **Initialize & Bind**: Every new project scaffolded must be mirrored to the `https://github.com/vrvish/` namespace. Agents must strictly adhere to the VRM taxonomy when naming and placing repositories.
2. **The API Sync Tool**: Agents must construct a structured JSON payload detailing the files changed, the precise commit message (following conventional commits), and POST this payload to the Swarm's internal Git Sync API (Implementation in WP-208).
3. **The `Stage Gate` Sync**: When Agent G audits a completed Work Package, the final step of the audit script MUST trigger the Git Sync API to cleanly stage, commit, and push the active T1 platform branch to the remote origin.

## 4. The Repository-Root Barricade Rule (GitIgnore Safety)
The Swarm MUST never inject `.gitignore` files nested deeply inside transient folders like `node_modules` or `.next`. Doing so forces Git to traverse massive cache trees and causes catastrophic CPU/RAM overload (Git Overload).

**The Rule:**
- Use **exactly one** `.gitignore` at the **git repository root** per repo. Do not scatter duplicate templates under `T6-*`, `theHive`, `scripts`, work packages, or caches.
- Stack-specific ignores must be **path-scoped** so legitimate source is never excluded by accident:
  - **SharePoint Framework:** all SPFx project roots live under a directory segment named `SPFx`. Ignore only SPFx build output via `**/SPFx/lib/`, `**/SPFx/temp/`, `**/SPFx/release/` (do not use repo-wide `/lib/`; do not add parallel `**/RSA_SPFx/**/lib/` when the build output already lives under `.../SPFx/...`).
  - **Power BI custom visuals (pbiviz):** host each visual project under a directory segment named `pbiviz`. Ignore only `**/pbiviz/.tmp/`, `**/pbiviz/package/`, and `**/pbiviz/**/*.pbiviz` (do not use repo-wide `/.tmp/` or `/package/` for pbiviz).
- Use **unanchored** patterns at repo root for artefacts that must never appear anywhere in the tree (e.g. `node_modules/`, `.next/`, `.env`), not leading-`/` copies pasted per subfolder.

### Canonical .gitignore Template (repository root)
```gitignore
# Repository root barricade (8002). One file per repo.
# SPFx: outputs only under .../SPFx/... | pbiviz: projects under .../pbiviz/...

# Secrets
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
.env.local
**/.env.local

# Certificates and keys
*.pfx
*.p12
*.pem
*.cert
*.key

# Dependencies and build output (any depth)
node_modules/
.next/
out/
build/
dist/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
pnpm-debug.log*

# SPFx — only under SPFx project roots
**/SPFx/lib/
**/SPFx/temp/
**/SPFx/release/
*.sppkg

# Power BI custom visuals — only under pbiviz project roots
**/pbiviz/.tmp/
**/pbiviz/package/
**/pbiviz/**/*.pbiviz

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
env/
Olive_House_Venv/

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
```

Add repository-specific lines only at the root when required (e.g. `theHive/.env` in `T1-vrvish-core`, Prisma `generated/` in a named app). Do not reintroduce nested `.gitignore` files for the same rules.

## 5. Mandatory Remote Repository Provisioning (The Parity Rule)

**This is a hard rule.** A local `git init` without first creating the repository on GitHub produces zero remote parity — the repo appears healthy locally but is invisible on GitHub. This is exactly the failure mode that caused `T1-vrvish-profile` to be missing for weeks.

**The Rule:**  
When any agent scaffolds a new platform or repository for the first time, it MUST provision the remote GitHub repository via **`Engine_Git_SourceControl.js`** before the first push. Do NOT use raw `git` CLI commands or raw PowerShell `curl` for this — use the engine.

### Canonical Usage:
```js
const Engine_Git_SourceControl = require('C:/GitHub/T1-vrvish-core/scripts/T0-Core/engines/Engine_Git_SourceControl');
const engine = new Engine_Git_SourceControl('C:/GitHub/MY-REPO-NAME');

// 1. Provision the remote repo on GitHub (uses GITHUB_PAT from .env)
await engine.createRemoteRepo('MY-REPO-NAME', { private: true });

// 2. Sync all local changes up
await engine.sync('feat(init): initial scaffold');
```

`createRemoteRepo()` calls the GitHub REST API (`POST /user/repos`) internally, stores the PAT from `.env`, and sets the remote origin automatically.

> [!CAUTION]
> Never assume a remote repo exists just because a `git remote -v` shows an origin URL. Always call `createRemoteRepo()` on first scaffold.